MISSION:
- Make exact map placement practical under a finger, let an operator plan one saved
  temporary paddock split without destructively replacing the parent pasture, preserve
  that exact split as a possible future permanent-boundary blueprint, and teach RangeWater
  gradually through brief, optional, offline guidance.

RELEASE CHARACTER:
- This is a precision, planning, and learnability release.
- It must preserve every accepted RangeWater 1.1.0 ranch record and behavior.
- It does not change the scientific meaning of the 800-foot preferred zone, the
  800-to-1,000-foot transition zone, recorded rest, or forage estimates.

AUTHORITY BOUNDARY:
- Michael ratified this complete Revision 1 contract on 2026-09-27.
- Application implementation, the specified schema 7-to-8 migration, archive format 3,
  automated verification, and signed field/internal-test artifacts are authorized.
- Google Play production publication is not authorized by this ratification.

🪨 BLOCK 2 — PROPOSED PRODUCT SCOPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The proposed RangeWater 1.2.0 scope contains six related capabilities:

1. Precision Placement Loupe.
2. Improved snapping and exact-coordinate placement feedback.
3. A non-destructive saved single-line paddock split plan.
4. A brief first-use welcome and optional rotating Field Tips during startup.
5. Optional, one-time contextual hints for major workflows.
6. A complete offline RangeWater Field Guide reachable from the existing overflow menu.

PROTECTED 1.1.0 BEHAVIOR:
- Existing pasture boundaries and canonical fence junctions remain authoritative.
- Existing gates, water assignments, herds, movements, circuits, roles, recorded-rest
  derivation, forage observations, backups, camera state, and layer preferences survive.
- Existing field and Play signing identities remain unchanged.
- No accepted record is silently reinterpreted as a paddock record.

🎮 BLOCK 3 — PRECISION PLACEMENT LOUPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3A. PURPOSE

- A fingertip hides the exact map point being chosen. During supported placement and
  dragging operations, RangeWater provides a magnified inset with a fixed crosshair so
  the operator can see the precise coordinate beneath the active pointer.

3B. SUPPORTED WORKFLOWS

The loupe is available while:
- drawing a new pasture;
- adding a corner to an existing pasture;
- moving an unshared pasture corner;
- moving an approved shared fence junction;
- placing or moving a water source;
- placing or moving a gate candidate on a fence segment; and
- placing either endpoint of a paddock split.

The loupe does not appear during ordinary map panning, zooming, asset inspection,
Herd Focus, circuit management, forage entry, backup, or restore.

3C. INTERACTION CONTRACT

- The operator presses and holds in a supported placement mode.
- The loupe appears above and horizontally offset from the finger so it remains visible.
- The loupe displays a live magnified view of the source map and relevant RangeWater
  overlays around the candidate coordinate.
- A fixed, high-contrast crosshair marks the coordinate that will be accepted.
- Moving the finger moves the candidate coordinate beneath the crosshair.
- Releasing the finger accepts the candidate into the current in-memory draft.
- ACTION_CANCEL, a system interruption, or leaving the placement mode accepts nothing.
- Existing Save, Cancel, and Undo controls remain authoritative. Loupe release is not a
  database save unless the accepted workflow already saves at that step.
- A quick ordinary tap remains supported where the accepted workflow already allows it.
  Press-and-hold supplies precision; it does not make every placement require a hold.

3D. VISUAL AND ACCESSIBILITY RULES

- The loupe must not cover the active finger, primary editing controls, MapLibre logo,
  provider attribution, or the selected coordinate when a safe alternate position exists.
- It chooses an above-left or above-right position from available screen space.
- The crosshair remains visible over bright aerial imagery and dark labeled imagery.
- The inset includes a visible border and must remain legible outdoors.
- TalkBack announces the active placement mode and whether the candidate is snapped.
- Haptic feedback may confirm a valid snap using ordinary Android feedback; there is no
  continuous vibration and no custom sound requirement.
- Mouse and trackpad users retain direct point-and-click placement without requiring a
  touch hold. The loupe may be shown during pointer drag when useful.

3E. COORDINATE TRUTH

- The accepted latitude/longitude is the coordinate represented by the crosshair.
- The loupe may not use a visually shifted coordinate while persisting the hidden finger
  coordinate or vice versa.
- Magnification is visual only and does not change the main map camera or saved viewport.
- Exiting precision placement restores the operator's settled camera exactly.

🎮 BLOCK 4 — IMPROVED SNAPPING AND PLACEMENT FEEDBACK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Existing 32-dp shared-junction snapping and accepted gate snapping remain the baseline.
- The loupe shows the active snap target and the exact unsnapped candidate before release.
- Shared-junction snap feedback uses the existing snapped-junction visual identity and
  the message "Snapped to shared corner."
- Fence placement shows the projected fence point, not the raw off-fence pointer point.
- Paddock endpoints must snap to the selected pasture's boundary.
- Water placement remains free-coordinate placement unless a future contract separately
  authorizes snapping water to another asset.
- Snap tolerance remains screen-space based where already accepted so phone and tablet
  interaction remains usable across zoom levels.
- The engine selects the nearest valid candidate within tolerance and never silently
  joins a second-best invalid target.
- Existing confirmations for joining or moving shared junctions remain mandatory.
- Existing gate clearance, overlap, planned-movement protection, and topology checks
  remain DAO-owned and cannot be bypassed by the loupe.

🎮 BLOCK 5 — SAVED TEMPORARY PADDOCK SPLIT PLAN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5A. PRODUCT MEANING

- User-facing feature name: Plan Paddock Split.
- A paddock split is a planning line representing proposed temporary polywire inside one
  mapped pasture.
- It is not a surveyed boundary, permanent fence, gate, movement route, circuit, herd
  location, or claim that wire exists in the field.
- The parent pasture remains one canonical PastureEntity.
- The split derives two planning regions and their map-estimated acreages without
  replacing, renaming, or duplicating the parent pasture.
- The saved plan is promotion-ready by design: a future separately ratified operation may
  reuse the same validated line and side labels to create permanent pasture boundaries
  without asking the operator to redraw the division.
- RangeWater 1.2.0 does not perform that promotion and does not imply that the temporary
  line has become a permanent fence merely because the plan was saved.

5B. INITIAL 1.2.0 GEOMETRY BOUNDARY

- One active saved split is allowed per pasture in 1.2.0.
- The split is one straight segment with two distinct endpoints.
- Both endpoints must snap to the selected pasture boundary.
- An endpoint may lie on a boundary segment or existing canonical corner.
- The segment must pass through the pasture interior and divide the parent polygon into
  exactly two valid, non-overlapping, positive-area regions.
- A tangent, boundary-following, zero-area, self-touching, or multi-result split is rejected.
- Each derived region must meet a small implementation safety threshold documented and
  tested before implementation; the UI must not invent a universal agricultural minimum.
- Curved lines, multi-bend polywire, multiple simultaneous cuts, and executing permanent
  conversion into child pastures remain outside 1.2.0.

5C. OPERATOR WORKFLOW

1. Select a pasture.
2. Choose Plan Paddock Split.
3. Read the brief explanation that the plan does not alter the pasture boundary.
4. Press, drag, or tap to place the first endpoint on the pasture boundary.
5. Place the second endpoint on another valid part of the boundary.
6. Review the line, both derived regions, acreage on each side, and reconciliation total.
7. Optionally swap or edit the suggested side labels.
8. Save or Cancel.

During editing:
- either endpoint may be moved with the precision loupe;
- Undo reverses the latest endpoint placement or movement;
- Cancel restores the last saved plan without a database write;
- Save remains disabled until the split is valid;
- validation messages explain whether an endpoint missed the boundary, the line failed
  to cross the interior, or the result did not produce exactly two safe regions.

5D. DISPLAY AND MANAGEMENT

- A saved split renders as a high-contrast temporary-polywire line visually distinct
  from permanent magenta pasture boundaries and orange gates.
- The two derived regions use subtle, distinguishable planning fills that do not obscure
  water coverage, herd badges, gates, or required attribution.
- Labels default to "Paddock A" and "Paddock B" and may be renamed.
- The pasture card shows the active plan and both map-estimated acreages.
- The operator may Edit, Archive, or Delete the plan with confirmation.
- Archived plans are retained for future reference but are not rendered as active plans.
- The delete confirmation warns that deletion also discards the reusable split blueprint;
  Archive is the recommended choice when the operator may want the line again later.
- No herd, circuit, movement, forage, rest, gate, or water record is automatically
  assigned to a derived paddock region in 1.2.0.

5E. BOUNDARY-EDIT SAFETY

- Paddock endpoints are stored against the parent pasture and its canonical boundary
  segment anchors rather than as detached screen pixels.
- Moving an endpoint never moves the permanent pasture boundary.
- Editing the parent boundary previews and revalidates its active paddock split.
- A boundary change that leaves the split valid may update the derived regions from the
  anchored endpoints in the same accepted save.
- A boundary change that would detach an endpoint or invalidate the two-region result is
  blocked until the operator moves, archives, or deletes the affected split.
- Deleting the parent pasture remains blocked by every existing accepted dependency and
  additionally requires explicit treatment of its paddock split.

5F. FUTURE PERMANENT-BOUNDARY PROMOTION SEAM

- The 1.2.0 plan must preserve enough durable identity, canonical boundary anchoring,
  side labeling, and validated geometry to let a later contract offer an action such as
  "Make Permanent Pastures" without redrawing the split.
- A future promotion would revalidate the parent boundary and saved split at the moment
  of conversion, then derive both proposed child boundaries from that accepted geometry.
- Promotion cannot be a blind one-tap mutation. Before committing, a future review must
  resolve at least:
  - permanent names for both resulting pastures;
  - which gates border or connect each resulting pasture;
  - which water assignments apply to each resulting pasture;
  - which Grazing Circuits include either or both resulting pastures;
  - the truthful location of any herd currently recorded in the parent pasture;
  - handling of planned movements involving the parent pasture; and
  - preservation of historical movements, forage acreage snapshots, rest derivation,
    and audit references that were created while the parent pasture existed.
- The likely safe future pattern is to retain or archive the original parent identity for
  historical references while creating new permanent child identities, but that policy
  is not ratified here and must be settled by the future conversion contract.
- A plan that is no longer geometrically valid after later boundary changes must be
  repaired before promotion. Promotion-ready does not mean validation may be skipped.
- Nothing in 1.2.0 prevents the rancher from continuing to use the plan as temporary
  polywire indefinitely; permanent promotion remains optional.

🪨 BLOCK 6 — PROPOSED PERSISTED DATA MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6A. PADDOCK SPLIT PLAN

Table: paddock_split_plans
Entity: PaddockSplitPlanEntity

Fields:
- id: Long, auto-generated primary key
- pastureId: Long, required, indexed
- name: String, required, trimmed
- sideALabel: String, required, default "Paddock A"
- sideBLabel: String, required, default "Paddock B"
- startJunctionAId: Long, required
- startJunctionBId: Long, required
- startSegmentRatio: Double, required, range 0.0 through 1.0
- endJunctionAId: Long, required
- endJunctionBId: Long, required
- endSegmentRatio: Double, required, range 0.0 through 1.0
- createdAt: Long, required
- updatedAt: Long, required
- archivedAt: Long?, nullable

Foreign keys:
- pastureId -> pastures.id, ON DELETE RESTRICT / NO ACTION
- each junction ID -> fence_junctions.id, ON DELETE RESTRICT / NO ACTION

Indices and constraints:
- indexed pastureId and all junction-reference fields;
- at most one row with archivedAt null per pasture, enforced by DAO-owned transaction
  validation because Room/SQLite portability cannot rely on one partial unique index;
- both ordered junction pairs must identify real consecutive boundary segments belonging
  to the referenced pasture;
- endpoint ratios must be finite and within the closed unit interval;
- endpoints and the derived split must pass Block 5 validation before insertion or update.

6B. DERIVED, NOT PERSISTED

- Endpoint coordinates are resolved from canonical junction coordinates plus the stored
  normalized segment ratios.
- Paddock A and B polygon coordinates are derived at runtime.
- Side acreages and percentages are derived at runtime.
- No duplicate child-pasture polygons or stored acreage totals are introduced.
- Historical forage observations retain their accepted parent-pasture acreage snapshots.
- The stored parent reference, endpoint segment anchors, side labels, timestamps, and
  stable plan ID form the durable seam for a future no-redraw promotion workflow.
- Future conversion code may derive proposed child geometry from this seam, but may not
  silently rewrite or reassign existing records without its own ratified migration and
  transaction contract.

6C. DEVICE-LOCAL HELP PREFERENCES

The following use a dedicated SharedPreferences repository and do not advance Room:
- fieldTipsEnabled: Boolean, default true
- contextualHintsEnabled: Boolean, default true
- welcomeSeen: Boolean, default false
- dismissedHintIds: Set<String>, default empty
- nextTipIndex: Int, default zero

These preferences are device-local usability state. They are not ranch records and are
not added to .rangewater archives in 1.2.0.

🎮 BLOCK 7 — WELCOME, STARTUP TIPS, AND REAL LOADING STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7A. FIRST USE

- On the first completed application launch, RangeWater briefly shows:
  "Welcome to RangeWater. Thanks for using it."
- Supporting line:
  "Map water access, pastures, gates, herds, grazing circuits, rest, and forage—all
  while keeping your ranch records on your device."
- The welcome transitions into the usable map without requiring an account, network
  request, or mandatory tutorial.
- It does not impose an artificial delay after the map is ready.
- The welcome can be replayed from Help & Field Guide.

7B. LATER STARTS

- When Field Tips are enabled, later launches show one compact rotating tip near the
  bottom of the map while startup work completes.
- The tip is non-modal: the map becomes usable as soon as it is ready.
- The tip may be dismissed immediately and fades after a baseline three seconds.
- Android's recommended accessibility timeout extends the display when the operator's
  accessibility settings require more reading time.
- The tip must not cover MapLibre attribution, provider attribution, bottom sheets, or
  primary editing controls.
- Tip rotation is deterministic and offline; no advertisement or remote content exists.

7C. LOADING TRUTH

- RangeWater may show short, accurate state messages only while that work is pending:
  - Loading ranch records
  - Preparing ranch map
  - Loading aerial imagery
  - Restoring saved map view
- A delayed imagery service must not be described as database work, and a completed
  stage must not continue displaying as active.
- No fake percentage, fake progress bar, or deliberate delay is allowed.
- Existing map failure and fallback behavior remains authoritative.

🖍️ BLOCK 8 — PROPOSED ROTATING FIELD TIPS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Initial bundled tips:

1. "Assign water sources to pastures to see accessible coverage."
2. "Create a portable backup before making major boundary changes."
3. "Grazing Circuits organize pastures but do not move a herd."
4. "Recorded rest is calculated from completed herd movements."
5. "Forage estimates are only as accurate as the samples entered."
6. "Press and hold during placement to use the precision loupe."
7. "Green shows preferred water access through 800 feet."
8. "Yellow shows the 800-to-1,000-foot planning transition."
9. "Map acreage is an estimate, not a surveyed boundary."
10. "A paddock split is a plan until the polywire is installed in the field."

Tip-content rules:
- Tips remain short enough for narrow portrait phones.
- Tips do not claim biological readiness, guaranteed forage, surveyed accuracy, or
  guaranteed livestock access.
- Tips use accepted RangeWater terms and units.
- Tips may be revised as public text without altering ranch data or calculation rules,
  but materially new advice requires product-owner review.

🎮 BLOCK 9 — CONTEXTUAL HINTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Contextual hints are enabled by default and may be turned off independently of startup
  Field Tips.
- Each hint is normally shown once per installation, then recorded by a stable hint ID.
- Every hint provides an immediate dismissal path.
- Help & Field Guide provides "Reset contextual hints."
- Resetting hints changes no ranch record or map setting.

Initial contextual hints:
- Draw Pasture: "Tap corners in order. Use Undo for mistakes and Finish to close it."
- Edit Boundary: "Select a corner to move it, or use Add Corner on a fence segment."
- Precision Loupe: "Press and hold, then move the map point beneath the crosshair."
- Add Gate: "Place the gate directly on a fence segment connected to this pasture."
- Water Assignment: "Accessible coverage includes only explicitly assigned pastures."
- Herd Movement: "Planning does not move the herd. Complete the movement when it occurs."
- Grazing Circuit: "A circuit is a planning group. Assigning it does not move the herd."
- Record Forage: "Measure several representative undisturbed points before entering an average."
- Paddock Split: "This plans temporary polywire and does not replace the pasture boundary."
- Data Safety: "Backups stored outside RangeWater can restore records after replacement or loss."

🌐 BLOCK 10 — OFFLINE RANGEWATER FIELD GUIDE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Entry point: existing overflow menu -> Help & Field Guide.
- The guide is bundled with the application and works without internet service.
- It uses searchable or clearly indexed phone-safe sections rather than one enormous
  scrolling wall of text.
- It includes a Close or Back path on every screen and preserves the underlying map state.

Required sections:
1. Getting Started
2. Reading the Map and Imagery
3. Drawing and Correcting Pastures
4. Precision Placement and Snapping
5. Planning a Paddock Split
6. Adding Gates
7. Adding Water and Understanding Coverage
8. Creating Herds and Recording Movements
9. Building Grazing Circuits
10. Understanding Recorded Rest
11. Recording Forage and Stockpile Observations
12. Creating, Restoring, and Sharing Backups
13. Common Problems and Recovery
14. Planning Limits and Important Disclosures

Required disclosures:
- Mapped boundaries and acreage are estimates, not surveys.
- Water coverage is a planning visualization and does not guarantee animal access.
- Gate-aware propagation is not part of 1.2.0.
- Rest is derived from recorded movements and does not prove biological recovery.
- Forage and stockpile values are operator-input estimates, not measured feed inventory.
- Paddock splits are planning records and do not prove that temporary fence exists.
- RangeWater remains local-first; uninstalling without an external backup can remove data.

Help controls:
- Field Tips: On / Off
- Contextual Hints: On / Off
- Show Welcome Again
- Reset Contextual Hints
- Open Field Guide

🔧 BLOCK 11 — DATABASE MIGRATION CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Add PaddockSplitPlanEntity.
- Advance RangeWaterDatabase from version 7 to version 8.
- Add explicit MIGRATION_7_8 after every accepted prior migration.
- Migration creates only the new empty paddock_split_plans table and its indices.
- Migration must not modify existing water, pasture, junction, vertex, assignment, gate,
  herd, movement, circuit, role, herd-circuit assignment, or forage-observation rows.
- Destructive migration and fallbackToDestructiveMigration remain prohibited.

Required migration evidence:
- Begin with a real schema-7 database populated with at least one valid record from every
  existing entity category.
- Upgrade through MIGRATION_7_8.
- Prove every existing row and relationship remains unchanged.
- Prove the new table is empty and writable.
- Prove foreign keys, active-plan validation, and invalid-anchor rejection after upgrade.

🔧 BLOCK 12 — ARCHIVE AND RESTORE CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- RangeWater 1.2.0 writes archive format version 3.
- The archive retains the .rangewater extension and accepted checksum design.
- Format 3 adds paddockSplitPlans and its record count.
- The reader accepts formats 1, 2, and 3.
- Format 1 upgrades in memory with empty 1.1 circuit/forage and 1.2 paddock collections.
- Format 2 upgrades in memory with an empty paddockSplitPlans collection.
- Version-specific record-count validation remains exact.
- Archives newer than format 3 are rejected before live writes.
- Validation rejects missing pasture or junction references, non-boundary segment pairs,
  non-finite or out-of-range ratios, duplicate active plans per pasture, invalid derived
  geometry, and unsupported archived-state values.
- Snapshot, replace-all, emergency rollback, and Undo Last Restore include paddock plans.
- Restore remains replace-all, validated before writing, foreign-key ordered, and atomic.

🔧 BLOCK 13 — DAO AND TRANSACTION RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Required operations:
- observe active and archived paddock plans;
- get a plan by ID or parent pasture;
- create one valid active plan per pasture;
- update name, labels, and anchored endpoints atomically;
- archive, reactivate when no active conflict exists, or delete with confirmation;
- report paddock plans that block a pasture or topology mutation;
- validate endpoint segment membership and derived two-region geometry inside the
  persistence transaction rather than trusting UI-only calculations.

Invariants:
- No invalid or partial paddock plan survives a failed save.
- UI preview geometry never writes before explicit Save.
- Cancel produces no database write.
- Failed archive, reactivation, endpoint update, restore, or boundary update leaves the
  previous state unchanged.
- Existing accepted pasture and gate transaction protections remain in force.

🔧 BLOCK 14 — GEOMETRY AND MAP-DERIVATION RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- JTS performs robust line/polygon noding and polygonization for the split result.
- Turf remains authoritative for displayed geodesic acreage, preserving accepted acreage
  semantics across parent and derived planning regions.
- The split engine must return exactly two interior polygonal regions.
- Region interiors do not overlap and their union reconciles with the parent pasture.
- Derived side acreage totals reconcile to parent mapped acreage within 0.5% numerical
  tolerance, matching the accepted coverage reconciliation standard.
- The line and derived regions are generated from the parent geometry and anchored
  endpoints every time they are displayed; no screen-space geometry is persisted.
- Existing map layer precedence remains readable. Selected assets, gate symbols, herd
  badges, water points, and required attribution cannot be hidden by paddock fills.
- Herd Focus may dim unrelated paddock plans but may not mutate or delete them.

🔧 BLOCK 15 — AUTOMATED ACCEPTANCE GATES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
JVM / UNIT TESTS:
1. Loupe pointer-to-crosshair conversion returns the exact accepted map coordinate.
2. Cancel and pointer interruption accept no candidate.
3. Snap selection chooses the nearest valid target within the configured tolerance.
4. A valid straight split creates exactly two valid regions.
5. Tangent, boundary-following, same-point, zero-area, and non-crossing lines are rejected.
6. Derived regions are disjoint and exhaustive within the required tolerance.
7. Side acreages reconcile with the parent pasture within 0.5%.
8. Boundary-anchor resolution tracks valid canonical junction movement.
9. Help preferences default on, persist across restart, and reset without touching data.
10. Tip rotation is deterministic and uses only bundled content.

ANDROID ROOM TESTS:
1. MIGRATION_7_8 preserves every schema-7 entity and relationship.
2. Paddock-plan schema, indices, and foreign keys match this contract.
3. Create, edit, archive, reactivate, and delete survive database close and reopen.
4. A second active plan for the same pasture is rejected atomically.
5. Invalid or foreign-pasture segment anchors are rejected.
6. Protected boundary edits cannot orphan an active paddock plan.
7. Failed multi-record operations roll back completely.

ARCHIVE TESTS:
1. Real format-1 and format-2 archives restore into schema 8 with empty paddock plans and
   every former record intact.
2. Format 3 round-trips every accepted record and paddock plan with stable IDs.
3. Record counts and SHA-256 integrity use the correct version-specific shape.
4. Malformed plan relationships and geometry are rejected before replacement.
5. Restore and Undo Last Restore preserve the complete pre-restore state.

UI / ACCESSIBILITY TESTS WHERE PRACTICAL:
1. Disabling Field Tips and contextual hints persists across restart.
2. Welcome is first-use only unless explicitly replayed.
3. The startup tip is non-modal and does not block a ready map.
4. Help opens and closes without changing the map camera or selection state.
5. Loupe and hint content expose meaningful accessibility descriptions.

BUILD GATES:
- Imagery-gateway regression suite passes.
- JVM tests pass.
- Android Room instrumentation tests pass.
- Stable field APK compiles and its certificate is verified.
- Play AAB compiles, signs, and matches the configured upload certificate.
- No private signing material is committed.

🎮 BLOCK 16 — PHYSICAL-GLASS ACCEPTANCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test devices:
- Michael's current Samsung phone
- Samsung Tab S7

Required sequence:
1. Upgrade an existing Play or approved field RangeWater 1.1.0 installation containing
   pastures, gates, water, herds, movements, circuits, roles, and forage observations.
2. Confirm all existing records, camera state, map controls, and accepted calculations survive.
3. Exercise the loupe while drawing a pasture, moving a corner, placing a gate, moving
   water, and placing paddock endpoints.
4. Confirm the crosshair coordinate—not the obscured finger position—is accepted.
5. Confirm snapping feedback and shared-corner confirmations remain truthful.
6. Create a valid paddock split and hand-check both displayed acreages against the parent.
7. Move both endpoints, use Undo, Cancel, and Save, then cold reopen and verify persistence.
8. Attempt invalid tangent, boundary-following, and non-crossing splits and verify clear rejection.
9. Edit the parent boundary and verify valid plans track safely while invalidating edits are blocked.
10. Confirm the plan changes no herd location, movement, circuit, water assignment, gate,
    rest result, or historical forage observation.
11. Verify first-use welcome, later rotating tips, immediate dismissal, and no artificial delay.
12. Turn Field Tips and contextual hints off, cold reopen, and confirm both remain off.
13. Replay the welcome, reset hints, and confirm no ranch data changes.
14. Read every Field Guide section in phone portrait, phone landscape, and tablet layout.
15. Create a format-3 backup, restore it on the other device, and verify the paddock plan.
16. Use Undo Last Restore and confirm the complete prior state returns.

Acceptance authority:
- CI proves compilation and automated contracts.
- Michael's physical-glass report is required before field acceptance.
- Google Play internal testing must prove the 1.1.0-to-1.2.0 in-place update preserves
  records before any production-promotion decision.
- Production publication requires a separate explicit instruction from Michael for the
  exact internally tested versionCode 20 bundle. No rebuild may be substituted silently.

🖍️ BLOCK 17 — HUMAN-EDITABLE CONTENT AND TUNING KNOBS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The following values may be tuned during draft review or device acceptance without
changing the ranch-data meaning:
- loupe diameter and visual magnification;
- crosshair line weight and contrast treatment;
- baseline startup-tip display duration, proposed at three seconds;
- tip ordering and concise wording;
- contextual-hint wording;
- Field Guide wording and section order;
- paddock-plan line and fill colors, provided permanent fences, gates, water coverage,
  selections, and attribution remain distinguishable.

The following are not mere tuning changes:
- permanent splitting of PastureEntity;
- more than one active split per pasture;
- multi-segment or curved polywire;
- automatic herd, circuit, water, forage, or rest assignment to a derived region;
- changing Room or archive versions;
- changing scientific thresholds or calculation meanings.

🪨 BLOCK 18 — EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- executing permanent conversion of one pasture into multiple PastureEntity records in
  RangeWater 1.2.0; the saved plan deliberately preserves a future no-redraw conversion path
- curved, freehand, or multi-bend polywire
- multiple simultaneous active cuts or automatic multi-paddock grids
- equal-acre automatic line solving
- automatic polywire length, reel count, post count, energizer, or cost estimates
- non-grazable holes, roads, woods, buildings, ponds, or land-cover classification
- gate-open/closed propagation of accessible water coverage
- live GPS, background location, or distance-to-water
- terrain, slope, DEM, hydraulic, or pipe-pressure analysis
- animal-demand, stocking-density, carrying-capacity, or days-of-feed calculations
- automatic rotation dates, forage growth, or biological readiness scoring
- cloud accounts, remote tips, advertisements, analytics, or user tracking
- parcel ownership lookup or SageWire Parcel Service integration
- iPhone or iPad implementation

These remain independent proposals. None may enter RW-TX-016-PMP without an explicit
scope revision and Michael's ratification.

